{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness of lotteries, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., negative outcomes) compared to favorable ones (i.e., positive outcomes). In this case, both lotteries have a negative outcome (-6000 or -3000 Euros) and a positive outcome (0 Euros). The unfavorable outcome is the loss, and the favorable outcome is zero (no loss). The key is to assess whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to the loss (unfavorable) versus the zero (favorable). However, in both lotteries, the probability of loss is very small: 0.1% for B and 0.2% for A. The probability of zero is 99.9% for B and 99.8% for A. Thus, in terms of probability mass, the loss is assigned to a smaller portion in both cases. Since the loss is only a small fraction of the total probability, the distribution is not right-skewed in the sense defined — rather, the majority of the probability mass is on zero (a neutral outcome). The reason as defined prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which would be one with a larger probability mass on the loss (unfavorable) relative to zero. But in this problem, both lotteries have a small probability of loss, and the loss is not disproportionately assigned. Therefore, the decision reason is not clearly applicable because neither lottery is right-skewed in the defined sense. The reason would only apply if one lottery had a significantly larger probability of loss (e.g., 50% loss), but here both have very low loss probabilities. Thus, the reason cannot be meaningfully applied to this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual states: 'es wird wohl bei beiden Optionen auf die 0 hinauslaufen, bei der extrem geringen Wahrscheinlichkeit des Verlusts nehme ich dann auf jeden Fall noch mal die geschmeidigeren 3.000'. This translates to: 'It is likely that both options will end up at zero; given the extremely low probability of loss, I will still take the more flexible 3000.' The individual acknowledges that both lotteries have a very low chance of loss (negative outcome), and that the outcome will mostly be zero. The individual expresses confidence in choosing one of the options despite the low risk, and refers to the option being 'geschmeidiger' (more flexible), which likely refers to a more favorable or smoother outcome (possibly implying a higher expected value or less risk). However, the individual does not mention skewness, probability distribution shape, or any comparison of the probability of loss versus zero. Instead, the justification centers on the low probability of loss and a preference for the option that feels more flexible (possibly implying a preference for a smaller loss or a better risk profile). There is no explicit or implicit reference to skewness, or to comparing the 'portion of probability' on unfavorable versus favorable outcomes. The deliberation process focuses on the low risk and a subjective sense of flexibility, not on distributional skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, whether one lottery has a greater portion of probability mass on unfavorable outcomes (losses) than the other. In this case, the loss probabilities are 0.1% for B and 0.2% for A. So A has a higher probability of loss (0.2% > 0.1%), meaning more probability mass is on the loss (unfavorable) outcome. According to the reason, a more right-skewed lottery has a greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Thus, A is more right-skewed than B. The reason would therefore prefer A. However, the individual’s verbal report does not mention skewness, nor does it reference the probability distribution of losses or zero outcomes. Instead, the individual focuses on the low probability of loss and the feeling of flexibility, suggesting a risk-averse or risk-neutral preference, possibly favoring the option with the smaller loss (which is not the case — A has a larger loss). The individual does not apply the skewness rule; instead, the justification is based on subjective experience and risk perception, not on the mathematical property of skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not used in the deliberation process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes. In this decision problem, Lottery A offers 500 Euros with 100% probability, meaning there is no uncertainty or risk — it is a certain outcome. Lottery B offers 1000 Euros with 50% probability and 0 Euros with 50% probability. This lottery has a 50% chance of a high gain and a 50% chance of zero. The unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) is equally weighted as the favorable one (1000 Euros), so the probability mass is evenly distributed between favorable and unfavorable outcomes. Therefore, Lottery B is not right-skewed (since unfavorable outcomes do not constitute a greater portion of probability), and in fact, it is symmetric. Lottery A has no unfavorable outcomes at all, so it is not right-skewed either. Thus, neither lottery is right-skewed, and the decision reason — which prefers the more right-skewed lottery — cannot be applied meaningfully here because both lotteries lack right skewness. The reason is not triggered by the structure of the lotteries in this case.",
